# CI, checks and the lint gate

## Don't manually trigger CI runs

Do not manually trigger or re-run CI workflows (a manual dispatch, a re-run of a failed run) unless the owner explicitly says to.
CI minutes are expensive, and the Main Health thread already watches `main` and reports a red build.
Every check workflow under `.github/workflows/` is triggered by manual dispatch only.

Two exceptions belong to the Main Health thread alone:

- **The scheduled full pass.** The owner's standing schedule has the Main Health thread dispatch every check workflow on `main` (twice a day by default).
  Each firing dispatches only if something has merged since the last pass.
- **A single re-run** when a failure looks infrastructural or possibly flaky.

Neither exception covers any other agent, or any extra run beyond them.

Because CI only runs twice a day, the gap between passes has no real-CI signal at all.
`scripts/local-full-suite.sh` is this project's own local stand-in for that gap: project-owned, not generated by the template, and its purpose, schedule and evidence format (`watch-main-ci`'s Routine 2) are a stage 0 task, not something left for Main Health to notice is missing.

## A ticket's own checks (Default)

The standing merge gate for an implementation ticket is to run the test files near the change, keep coverage of the touched modules from dropping, clear the review gate (`.forge/review-gate.md`), and self-merge.
Never write a ticket's "done" criteria as "dispatch CI", "link a green run", "run the full suite", or "wait on a review beyond the standing gate".
Those cost CI minutes or a human's time that only the Main Health thread's scheduled passes and the standing review gate are budgeted for.
A specific brief or the owner can ask for a real CI run or extra review on a specific ticket when the work genuinely needs it, such as a migration that can only be validated against a real database, but only explicitly on that ticket.
`to-tickets` and `triage` phrase checks as "run the tests near the change; coverage of touched modules must not drop".

## The coverage floor is part of finishing a PR

The main test suite has a coverage floor ({{ coverage_floor }}%, the `coverage_floor` answer in `.copier-answers.yml`, which stage 0 sets from a measured baseline), enforced by the suite's own configuration whenever the full suite runs.
The floor only ratchets up; raising it needs no approval, and lowering it needs the owner's.
A change to the floor changes the suite's configuration and that answer in the same PR (`copier update --trust --defaults --skip-tasks --vcs-ref <_commit> --data coverage_floor=<n>`, with `_commit` from `.copier-answers.yml` so the TheForge version does not move).
`scripts/test_coverage_floor_answer.py` holds this sentence's stated percentage equal to the `.copier-answers.yml` answer, the same way `scripts/test_check_module_size.py` holds the size caps; the suite's own configuration file is stage 0's own choice of test tool and does not exist in the template, so keeping that third value in step with the other two stays this PR process, not a mechanical check.
`--defaults` is required in a non-interactive session: without it Copier re-asks every other question, and with no TTY to answer them it dies with `EOFError` instead of applying this one change; with it, every other answer keeps its previous value from `.copier-answers.yml`.
Because the full suite runs on the Main Health thread's schedule rather than per PR, a PR protects coverage locally.
A PR that adds code with real decisions in it (branches, error handling, retries, state transitions) tests those paths itself, and checks coverage of the modules it touched before merging.
A coverage regression that lands on `main` is a gate outage for everyone else's PRs, not a minor clean-up.

## Lint is enforced by the pre-commit hook, not CI

The git pre-commit hook (`.pre-commit-config.yaml`) is the real lint gate.
Install it once per clone with `pre-commit install --install-hooks`; the session-start hook does this automatically where the harness runs it.
Never commit with `--no-verify` to get past it: fix what it flags instead.
Before assuming the hook ran, check it is installed (`test -f .git/hooks/pre-commit`).

The project's own commands, set at stage 0:

- setup: `{{ setup_command }}`
- full test suite: `{{ test_command }}`
- lint and format check: `{{ lint_command }}`

## Operational constants

`docs/agents/operational-constants.md` inventories the project's limits, intervals and tuning values, saying which are deploy-time configuration and which are deliberately hardcoded invariants.
A PR that adds or changes such a value updates that inventory in the same PR.

## Deployment configuration lives in the repo (Never)

The project's own deployment and environment configuration (configuration defaults and templates, images, the devcontainer, workflows) lives in this repo.
No agent changes a running deployment's configuration in place; any change is a ticketed PR to the repo's own file, redeployed from there.
